<img src="https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/Emilie_Chatelet_portrait_by_Latour.jpg" alt="Portrait of Émilie du Châtelet, attributed to Maurice Quentin de La Tour" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-8" loading="lazy" width="866" height="1037" />

<p>She held the highest qualification a woman could hold in eighteenth-century French science, and it was called nothing. There was no degree, no chair, no fellowship, no seat. The Academy of Sciences sold tickets to its public sessions and admitted women as spectators, on the correct side of an invisible line, and that was the ceiling.</p>

<p>So Émilie du Châtelet built the institution she could not join, inside a house in the Champagne countryside, out of hired tutors, imported books and letters. She learned what she needed in whatever language it was written in — Latin for the ancients and for Newton, Italian for the Italian mathematicians, German for Leibniz's followers, English for the philosophers and the satirists. And then she did the thing that a member of the Academy would have done: she turned the hardest science book in Europe into French and added two volumes of corrections to it.</p>

<p>Her story is usually told as a footnote to Voltaire. The more useful version is a case study in what self-education actually consists of when the doors are shut: not solitary genius, but an engineered environment, a hired teacher, a language learned on purpose, and a translation project used as a forcing function.</p>

<h2>Paris, 1706: A Daughter Given a Son's Education</h2>

<p>Gabrielle Émilie Le Tonnelier de Breteuil was born in Paris on 17 December 1706, the only daughter among six children of Louis Nicolas le Tonnelier de Breteuil and Gabrielle Anne de Froulay. Her father belonged to the lesser nobility and served at the court of Louis XIV as Principal Secretary and Introducer of Ambassadors — a role that meant foreign envoys passed through his house.</p>

<p>He also held a weekly salon at the family home, where writers and scientists were among the guests. When Louis XIV died in 1715, the baron retired from court and the family stayed in Paris, which kept the household inside the intellectual traffic of the capital. Her early education is not well documented and the sources carry real uncertainty, as scholars of her life point out. What is documented is that her father arranged unusual things for a girl: fencing and riding lessons, tutors brought in, and — when she was ten years old — a conversation partner who happened to be one of the most senior scientists in France.</p>

<p><strong>Bernard le Bovier de Fontenelle</strong>, the permanent secretary of the Académie des Sciences and the author of a celebrated book explaining astronomy to a general audience, was asked to discuss astronomy with the ten-year-old Émilie. Accounts also disagree about her mother, some saying she actively opposed her daughter's intellectual interests and threatened to send her to a convent, others that she encouraged the girl's habit of questioning what she was told. Both versions survive in the record, which is itself informative: a gifted daughter in 1716 was a contested project even inside her own family.</p>

<h2>By Twelve, Four Languages — and a Childhood Built Around a Library</h2>

<p>By the age of twelve she was fluent in Latin, Italian, Greek and German. Some accounts put the tally at six languages by that age and several modern encyclopedias repeat the larger number; the four are the ones the scholarship reliably agrees on. She later translated Greek and Latin plays and philosophy into French as exercises, studied mathematics, literature and science, and acquired the full accomplishment package of an aristocratic girl — dancing, harpsichord, singing, acting.</p>

<p>Two details from that childhood are worth keeping. The first is that she was allowed, on her father's insistence, to be taught like a son; the second is what she did with the money. As a teenager she used mathematics to work out gambling odds, because mathematics was the mechanism by which she could buy books.</p>

<h2>1725: A Marriage, and the Arithmetic of Freedom</h2>

<p>On 12 June 1725, at eighteen, she married the Marquis Florent-Claude du Chastellet-Lomont, thirty-four years old and a soldier who was frequently away. It was an arranged marriage of the kind normal to her class, and as a wedding gift her husband's father made him governor of Semur-en-Auxois in Burgundy; the couple moved there at the end of September.</p>

<p>The spelling of her name is a small historical accident: her husband's family name was <em>Chastellet</em>, and the familiar <em>Châtelet</em> came into use later, largely through Voltaire's writing. She had three children over the following years. After the third, she and her husband reached an understanding unusual for the period — they would live separate lives while maintaining a single household. That agreement is the hinge on which the rest of her life turns. It gave a married woman of the nobility something close to control of her own hours, and she spent the next quarter-century buying the education she had been structurally denied.</p>

<h2>The Café Gradot Story, and the Caveat It Deserves</h2>

<p>The most repeated anecdote about her is this. At the Café Gradot in Paris, a coffeehouse where men gathered for mathematics and argument, she arrived to join one of her teachers and was refused entry — a woman, in male space. She went home, changed into men's clothing, came back, and was admitted.</p>

<p>It is a good story, and the scholar who wrote the most recent philosophical study of her life, Andrew Janiak, rates it as probably a fairy tale. What is not in doubt is her own description of the wall she was trying to get past, written years later in the preface to her translation of Mandeville, where she asks why the most enlightened nations of Europe reserve thought for one sex:</p>

<blockquote>I feel the full weight of prejudice that excludes us so universally from the sciences, and it is one of the contradictions of this world which has always astonished me the most, that there are great countries where the law permits us to decide our destiny, but none where we are brought up to think. — <strong>Émilie du Châtelet, preface to her translation of Mandeville's The Fable of the Bees</strong></blockquote>

<p>And in an earlier draft of the same preface, she was blunter still:</p>

<blockquote>If I were king, I would establish colleges for women. — <strong>Émilie du Châtelet, draft preface to The Fable of the Bees</strong></blockquote>

<h2>1733: Mathematics, in Earnest, With Hired Teachers</h2>

<p>At twenty-six she resumed her mathematical studies, and here the "self-taught" label needs to be handled honestly. She was not a lone autodidact in a garret. She was a woman with money and a house, and she spent both on instruction, because instruction was the only route available to her.</p>

<ul>
  <li>From 1733 she studied algebra and calculus with <strong>Pierre-Louis Moreau de Maupertuis</strong>, a mathematician and member of the Academy of Sciences, who introduced her to Newton.</li>
  <li>In 1734 she hired <strong>Samuel König</strong>, a protégé of Maupertuis, to teach her algebra for three hours a day.</li>
  <li>By 1735 she was studying with <strong>Alexis Clairaut</strong>, one of the leading mathematicians of the century.</li>
  <li>She corresponded with the Swiss mathematicians <strong>Johann II Bernoulli</strong> and <strong>Leonhard Euler</strong>, and with Frederick II of Prussia.</li>
</ul>

<p>Seventy-six of her letters to Maupertuis survive, from 1734 to 1744, beginning with a student asking for help and ending with a peer arguing about gravity, matter, free will and the vis viva controversy. The database of her working life is a correspondence syllabus.</p>

<h2>She Learned English for a Reason, Not for a Life Skill</h2>

<p>French was the intellectual language of her world, Latin was the language of the ancients, and Newton had written his masterpiece in Latin — so why learn English at all? Because the argument was happening in English. Mandeville's <em>The Fable of the Bees</em>, which had been banned in France, was one of the books she wanted to answer, and Locke and the English philosophy she was reading existed in that language first.</p>

<p>Voltaire, who was closer to this episode than anyone, later boasted about her speed with numbers that do not agree with each other. In one version he claimed she learned to understand English in fifteen days. In another, in prose written to introduce her work, he said: "I taught her English. In about three months, she understood it as well as I did." Both are Voltaire, which is the only reliable thing about them. What the record does support is the goal-directedness of the learning: she did not acquire English as a general accomplishment. She acquired enough of it, fast, to read specific books and argue with them — the sharpest possible motive for a language learner to study.</p>

<p>Her translation of the <em>Fable of the Bees</em> was begun around 1734 or 1735 and worked on into the late 1730s. She chose not to render Mandeville's verse, on the stated grounds that she had no talent for verse, and translated the prose and the moral argument. It was not a neutral exercise. Where Mandeville's English insists that humans and animals share the same vital spirits, her French removes the phrase; her version refers to the human body alone. A translation can carry an argument in the parts it declines to reproduce.</p>

<p>The translation was never published in her lifetime. It sat in manuscript for more than two centuries and was finally printed by the Voltaire scholar Ira O. Wade in 1967 — which means the preface argument for women's education, one of the finest things she wrote, spent two hundred years in a drawer.</p>

<h2>1738: Fire, and the First Time the Academy Printed a Woman's Work</h2>

<p>In 1737 the Royal Academy of Sciences in Paris announced its 1738 prize competition: explain what fire is and how it propagates. Women could attend the Academy's sessions as audience members but could not take part in the institution on equal terms with men, and the competition was nonetheless formally open. Du Châtelet began investigating the question while Voltaire was preparing his own entry at Cirey. They started together, she came to disagree with several of his ideas, and she wrote her own submission without telling him.</p>

<p>Her <em>Dissertation sur la nature et la propagation du feu</em> treated fire as a distinct form of matter — as most natural philosophers of the period did — but argued it had no weight, against Voltaire's claim that it did. She examined the relation between fire and light and suggested that stars other than the Sun might produce colors not present in sunlight on Earth. Neither she nor Voltaire won; the Academy's judges nonetheless ranked both among the best entries, and when the authors asked for their work to be printed, the Academy agreed to publish them alongside the winning essays. Her dissertation appeared in the collection in 1739, aged thirty-two — the first work by a woman that the Academy published. She revised it in 1744, leaning harder on Leibniz while keeping much of Newton.</p>

<h2>1740: A Physics Textbook, Allegedly for a Thirteen-Year-Old</h2>

<p><em>Institutions de Physique</em> appeared in 1740, revised in 1742, and it is a strange and revealing book. It was framed as a review of the new science for the education of her thirteen-year-old son — and it is, in fact, a systematic synthesis attempting to reconcile the two great and mutually hostile systems of the day, Newton's and Leibniz's, with chapters on knowledge, God, space, time, matter and force.</p>

<p>Two features of it belong to the theme of this website. The first is that a woman barred from lecturing wrote her physics as a textbook for a pupil, which is how several self-taught scholars of the period smuggled their teaching into print. The second is what happened next. Samuel König, who had been paid to teach her algebra, publicly claimed that the <em>Institutions</em> were simply a recapitulation of his lessons. She was furious, and the dispute became one of the celebrated quarrels of the period; the book's reputation survived it, and in 1746 she was made a member of the Academy of Sciences of the Institute of Bologna.</p>

<p>Voltaire, who watched her calculate, left one biographical sentence that says more about her mind than any summary:</p>

<blockquote>I saw her, one day, divide a nine-figure number by nine other figures, in her head, without any help, in the presence of a geometer unable to keep up with her. — <strong>Voltaire, quoted in biographical accounts of du Châtelet</strong></blockquote>

<p>She also published a point-by-point rebuttal in 1741 of the Academy's secretary, Dortous de Mairan, on how the force of a moving body should be measured — and he withdrew from the controversy. Immanuel Kant's first publication, in 1747, engaged with her pamphlet. In later writing on the beautiful and the sublime, Kant sneered that a woman with a head full of Greek "might as well also wear a beard". The argument she had made in a physics dispute had reached the most important philosopher in Germany, who repaid her by writing about her sex instead of her work.</p>

<h2>1745 to 1749: The Principia, and Seventeen-Hour Days</h2>

<p>Her masterpiece was a translation. In 1745 she began work on Newton's <em>Philosophiae Naturalis Principia Mathematica</em>, the Latin treatise that had rewritten the physical universe, and she approached it as a scholar rather than a stylist.</p>

<ul>
  <li>She mastered calculus and reworked the analytic geometry that Newton's geometric proofs presupposed.</li>
  <li>She converted Newton's difficult geometric demonstrations into the modern mathematical notation her French readers could follow.</li>
  <li>She corrected Newton where the science had moved on, using Clairaut's work on the differing densities of the planets and Daniel Bernoulli's on the tides.</li>
  <li>She derived what is now called the conservation of energy from Newton's mechanics, and stated the squared relationship of mass and velocity that scholars have traced forward to the square in <code>E=mc²</code>.</li>
  <li>She worked, by the account of her biographers, up to seventeen hours a day on it.</li>
</ul>

<p>Newton had written in Latin because Latin was the common language of scholars — and Voltaire's preface to her edition makes the counterargument that Latin was also the problem: too few words for modern mathematics and physics, which is why a French text could spread the new science further than the Latin original had. This is the classic reason for translation, and the reason a translator is not a lesser author. Her commentary ran to almost two-thirds of the second volume. When I. Bernard Cohen, the twentieth century's leading Newton scholar, produced his own English edition, the only translation in any language he leaned on was the French one published by a woman who had been dead for two centuries.</p>

<h2>The Last Year: A Race Against a Date She Could See Coming</h2>

<p>In 1748, at forty-one, she began a relationship with the poet and soldier Jean François de Saint-Lambert while staying at Lunéville in the Duchy of Lorraine, in the orbit of Stanisław Leszczyński. She remained close to Voltaire, who accompanied her on those stays. Then, more than sixteen years after the birth of her previous child, she became pregnant again.</p>

<p>She had already written that she did not expect to survive another childbirth, and she said so in her letters. So she did what a person does when arithmetic gives her a deadline: she worked faster. By January 1749 she had finished the preface to the <em>Principia</em>; in May she was still at it, working with Clairaut. She gave birth to a daughter, Stanislas-Adélaïde, on 4 September 1749 at Lunéville. Six days later, at about one o'clock in the morning on 10 September, she died, aged forty-two. The daughter did not survive her long; the child died in Lunéville in May 1751, not yet two years old.</p>

<p>Three paragraphs of philosophy sit at the end of this biography that she wrote for herself — her <em>Discours sur le bonheur</em>, a discourse on happiness, in which she argued that the capacity to be happy is something a person can build, and that the obstacles to it are mostly internal. She did not publish it. It was printed in 1779, thirty years after her death.</p>

<h2>What She Left Behind</h2>

<p>Her translation of the Principia appeared posthumously: a preliminary edition in 1756, seven years after her death, and the complete edition in 1759, ten years after. It remains the only complete French translation of Newton's masterpiece and is still the standard one. A minor planet carries her name; so does a crater on Venus. She has been the subject of three plays and a Kaija Saariaho opera, and on 17 December 2021 Google marked her 315th birthday with a doodle.</p>

<p>None of that was the point of her life's argument, which she made in a preface almost nobody read for two hundred years. The point was that a talent like hers does not need to be created by institutions; it needs to be left alone, and in eighteenth-century France it was not. What she had instead was money, a house, hired tutors, a library, a correspondence, and an unbounded willingness to learn a language because a book she needed was written in it.</p>

<p>That is the practical inheritance. She did not wait to be ready, admitted, or certified. She picked the hardest text in her field, taught herself what it required, and shipped a version the specialists still use. For anyone learning a language in order to reach something on the other side of it, the lesson is exact: choose the book you cannot yet read, and let the effort of reaching it be the syllabus.</p>

